## 1. `resolvePaymentResult` — Return Shape Roulette (L21–32, agent_risk 0.44)

Evidence: *"3 object-literal return shapes"*, *"lowest key overlap: 0%"*, *"example keys: `{ code, message, ok }` vs `{ permanent, url }`"*.

**What an agent gets wrong today.** `ok` appears on the error branch (`false`) and the success branch (`true`), but **not at all** on the redirect branch. There is no return type annotation, so TypeScript infers a bare union and the natural caller idiom silently misclassifies:

```ts
if (result.ok) { /* success */ } else { /* treated as failure */ }
```

A redirect has `ok === undefined`, which is falsy — so every redirect falls into the failure path. An agent reading only lines 29 and 31 would conclude `ok` is the discriminant and write exactly that. Worse, an agent asked to add a fourth branch (say a 3DS challenge) will pattern-match on line 31, the closest return, and write `{ ok: true, … }` — marking a pending state as a completed payment.

Secondary hazard: the input bag is all-optional, so the error branch can legitimately return `{ ok: false, code: undefined, message: undefined }`. `code` is typed `string | undefined` at every call site.

**Refactor** — a real discriminated union, plus an explicit annotation so the contract can't drift again:

```ts
export type PaymentResult =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string; permanent: boolean }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string; cached: boolean };

type PaymentInput =
  | { kind: "error"; code: string; message: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url: string }
  | { kind: "success"; value: string };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  switch (input.kind) {
    case "error":    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
    case "redirect": return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: false };
    case "success":  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
  }
}
```

This maps directly onto the detector's `fix_shape` — *"pick one return shape; use a discriminated union if you need both"*. Discriminating on `kind` rather than `ok` means the switch is exhaustive, adding a fourth variant becomes a compile error at every caller instead of a silent fallthrough, and the required-vs-optional question (`code` when `kind === "error"`) is answered by the type rather than by reading the body.

Narrowing the *input* to a union too is the part that stops the crime recurring: with the current all-optional bag, `{ error: true, redirect: true }` is representable and the function resolves it by line order, which is a rule nobody wrote down.

