## 2. `describeCheckout` — Option Bag Junk Drawer (L10–19, agent_risk 0.39)

Evidence: *"parameter: options"*, *"6 distinct property reads: currency, plan, region, retry, role, status"*.

**What an agent gets wrong today.** There *is* a named `CheckoutOptions` interface, so this isn't an untyped bag — the crime is that six fields sit in one flat record and five of them are bare `string`. The function isn't describing anything; it's building a colon-delimited composite key where **field order is load-bearing and undocumented**. An agent asked to "add locale to the checkout description" will add `locale: string` to the interface and append `options.locale` to the array — the obvious edit, and it changes the format of every key this function has ever produced. If those keys are cache entries or idempotency keys, that's a silent cache-wide invalidation with no type error.

The five bare `string` fields also mean `plan` and `role` are mutually assignable. Swapping them at a construction site is invisible to the compiler and produces a plausible-looking key.

**Refactor** — group into cohesive records, narrow the strings, and make the key order explicit and named:

```ts
type PlanTier = "free" | "pro" | "enterprise";
type CheckoutRole = "owner" | "admin" | "member";
type CheckoutStatus = "pending" | "paid" | "failed";

interface CheckoutIdentity { plan: PlanTier; role: CheckoutRole }
interface CheckoutLocale   { region: string; currency: string }
interface CheckoutRuntime  { status: CheckoutStatus; retry: boolean }

export interface Checkout {
  identity: CheckoutIdentity;
  locale: CheckoutLocale;
  runtime: CheckoutRuntime;
}

/** Field order is part of the key format — changing it invalidates existing keys. */
export function describeCheckout({ identity, locale, runtime }: Checkout): string {
  return [
    identity.plan, identity.role,
    locale.region, locale.currency,
    runtime.status, String(runtime.retry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

This is the detector's `fix_shape` verbatim — *"split the options bag into named, narrowly-typed records"* — and the `name_option_shape` action's *"destructure only the fields this function owns"*. Destructuring at the signature is the cheap half: it puts all six reads in one place, so the next agent sees the full contract in the signature instead of scanning the body. The union types are the half that stops `plan`/`role` swaps. The comment is doing the work the type system can't: if key stability actually matters here, the stronger version is a `CHECKOUT_KEY_FIELDS` constant plus a test pinning a known input to a known output string, so an appended field fails a test rather than a code review.

